  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1052 AM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThe amplified upper ridge continues to push east into the \nTennessee Valley region Saturday night into Sunday. This is a \nfairly amplified upper ridge for this time of year. Models \nforecast bias lends itself towards cooler high temperatures than \nrealized in this type of setup. However, cooler/drier air from the\neast via a decaying wedge front could temper highs towards \ncurrent guidance. Expect highs to only reach the lower 70s on \nSunday under sunny skies. \n\nLows with drier air advecting into the region behind the wedge \nfront and light winds will likely allow lows to drop into the \nlower to mid 40s Sunday night. Sunday night into Monday the \noverall pattern begins to change. An upper low moving from the \nRockies eastward into portion of Nebraska, really starts to de- \namplify northern extent of the upper level ridge extending north \ninto the Ohio Valley region. However, it will still be fairly \namplified for this time of year. We will need to watch for mixing \nof drier air aloft to the surface during the day. This could push \nafternoon RH values close to or below 25% in some areas. \n\nThe center of the upper ridge also shifts east on Monday. This \nshould put an end to the effects of the wedge front. Highs could \nbe a bit higher than guidance Monday because of this. Current\nguidance is forecasting highs remaining between 70 and 75 \ndegrees. Higher temperatures based on upper level ridging could \npush temperatures a bit higher into the mid 70s at least. We will \nneed to watch for mixing of drier air aloft to the surface during \nthe day. This could push afternoon RH values close 25% in some \nareas.\n\nAs the upper low shifts eastward through the middle of next week,\nmodels really suppress the upper ridge well south into the Gulf \nof America. Return flow around the surface high off the eastern \nGulf of America and WSW flow aloft should allow for a bit of a \nwarmup. Highs should climb back into the mid to upper 70s.",
